\date{2026-07-28T17:20:40Z}
\import{macros}

\taxon{Example}
\title{the binary tetrahedral subgroup of the quaternions}

\tag{math}
\tag{fgap}
\tag{group}
\tag{quaternion}
\tag{semidirect-product}

\p{Inside #{\mathbb{H}^{\times}}, let
##{
T=Q\vee C.
}
The Hurwitz action in [[fgap-000C]] says that #{C} normalizes #{Q}. Hence
[[fgap-000G]] gives
##{
T=QC,\qquad Q\mathrel{\trianglelefteq}T.
}
}

\p{The groups #{Q} and #{C} have orders 8 and 3. The order of their
intersection divides both, so
##{
Q\cap C=\{1\}.
}
They are therefore complementary in #{T}. Every element has a unique normal
form
##{
q\omega^r,\qquad q\in Q,\quad 0\leq r<3,
}
and the internal semidirect-product theorem gives
##{
Q\rtimes_{\alpha}C\cong T,\qquad
\alpha(c)(q)=cqc^{-1}.
}
}

\p{The action orientation is fixed by the quaternion calculation:
##{
i\longmapsto k\longmapsto j\longmapsto i.
}
Voight identifies this semidirect product with the Hurwitz unit group and the
binary tetrahedral group in
\citet{sec. 11.2.4, p. 168}{voight2021quaternion}.}
